.TH CVEC_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_declare_scoped \- declare a vector variable destroyed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_declare_scoped(v, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable named v for elements of the given
type, exactly as cvec_declare(3) does, and adds a cleanup handler that
destroys the vector when control leaves the scope around it. The macro
creates no vector: it initializes v to NULL, and v stays NULL until
cvec_init(3) or cvec_init_mp(3) gives it a vector.

The NULL value in v is what makes the cleanup handler safe here, because
the handler runs on every exit from the scope, including an exit before
anything assigns the variable. A scoped declaration that left the variable
indeterminate would give an arbitrary value to the cleanup handler on any
early return between the declaration and the creation. Both companion type
variables start at NULL for the same reason, so nothing in the block reads
an indeterminate object.

The destruction happens on every path out of the block: the closing brace,
a return, a break, a continue, or a goto out of the block. Because the
macro sets the variable to NULL as it destroys the vector, an explicit
call to cvec_destroy(3) earlier in the block is safe and frees nothing
twice.

When you can create the vector on the same line as the declaration, use
cvec_construct_scoped(3) or cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3) instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Name of the vector variable to declare
type  Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
      Any type name is accepted, including a function
      pointer type such as void (*)(int) and an array
      type such as int[4]. For an array element type,
      give cvec_push(3) an array or a string literal.
      The macro copies its bytes into the element
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro is built on __attribute__((cleanup)), so it needs GCC or
Clang. On a toolchain that does not have that attribute, use
cvec_declare(3) with an explicit cvec_destroy(3).
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations and needs a semicolon at
the end of the call site, so it belongs where declarations are legal and
cannot be the body of an if or a loop without braces.
A block that leaves v at NULL is correct, because the cleanup handler
does nothing for a NULL vector.
When the vector holds pointers to heap buffers, the macro does not free
the elements one by one, so free the element data before you leave the
scope.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The vector must not live longer than its scope: do not return v, and do
not store it anywhere that code reads after the end of the block. When
the ownership must leave the block, use cvec_declare(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void load(bool use_pool, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *pool) {
    cvec_declare_scoped(vec, int);

    if (use_pool) {
        cvec_init_mp(vec, pool);
    } else {
        cvec_init(vec);
    }

    cvec_push(vec, 42);
}   /* vec is destroyed here, and on every earlier exit from the block */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3)
